nning the knight.\n\nReason:  \nIt immediately pressures Black’s position, pins the knight on f6 to the king (assuming Black hasn’t castled, which is likely given unmoved king and rook), and threatens to double Black’s pawns if exchanged. This creates long-term weaknesses and increases attacking chances, especially important if White is down material.\n\nAnswer: B: c1-g5'}
