e king in check?\n\nFrom e1, the queen on e1 can move along the e-file or first rank.\n\ng2 is on the same rank (rank 2)? No — e1 and g2 are not aligned.\n\ne1 → g2: difference of +2 files, +1 rank — that’s a knight move, not a queen line. So not directly attacking.\n\nQueen on e1 controls:\n- Entire e-file (e2, e3, ..., e8)\n- Entire first rank (a1, b1, ..., h1)\n\ng2 is not on e-file or first rank — so king on g2 is **not in check**.\n\nBut e2 is attacked — so if White ever wants to play a piece to e2, it would be under threat.\n\nNow, **K: g2-g3** — moving to g3 — is g3 attacked?\n\ng3: is it on e-file? No. On first rank? No. Diagonal from e1? e1 to g3: e1 → f2 → g3, so yes: diagonal of two squares — so e1-g3 is a diagonal of slope 1 — yes, e1 to g3 is two up, two right — so same color diagonal.\n\nDistance: e1 to g3 is +2 files, +2 ranks — so yes, on a diagonal. So **QBF1 on e1 attacks g3**.\n\nTherefore, **K: g2-g3** would move the White king into check — **illegal**.\n\nSo **K: g2-g3** is **not a legal move** — it must be removed.\n\nBut it was listed as a valid move — contradiction.\n\nPossibility: the move list is generated correctly, so perhaps our analysis is wrong.\n\nWait — is e1 to g3 a diagonal?  \ne1 = (5,1), g3 = (7,3) → Δfile = +2, Δrank = +2 → yes, same ratio — so **bishop move**, so queen can attack along that diagonal.\n\nBut only if no pieces block.\n\nIs there any piece between e1 and g3?\n\nSquares: e1, f2, g3.\n\nWhat is on f2? From the board:\n\nRank 2: f2 = PWF0 → White pawn.\n\nSo f2 is occupied by a White pawn — so the diagonal from e1 to g3 is blocked by the pawn on f2.\n\nTherefore, **QBF1 on e1 does NOT attack g3** — blocked by pawn on f2.\n\nSo g3 is **not under attack** — so **K: g2-g3** is **safe** from the queen.\n\nAny other attackers?\n\nBlack has:\n- Knight on f6 — attacks e4, d5, g4, h5 — not g3.\n- Pawns: none near — closest is on f7, g7 — no.\n- Bishop on c8 — but c8 to g3? c8 to g3 is long diagonal, but many pieces in betwee